WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.350
The culmination of the entire Marvel Cinematic
Universe thus far premiered Monday, April

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.010
23rd in Los Angeles.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:11.370
Those lucky enough to attend the momentous
first global screening of Avengers: Infinity

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:15.810
War have taken to social media to spill their
spoiler-free thoughts on the biggest cinematic

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.530
event of the decade.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:22.560
Entertainment Weekly's Anthony Breznican reacted
to Infinity War using only a string of cryptic

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.140
emojis, while Emmy-winning journalist Kelley
L. Carter simply tweeted, "I. Am. Shook."

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.830
But others had more tantalizing things to
say about the film.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:35.449
Most reactions have been positive thus far,
noting both the humor and the heart of Infinity

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:39.899
War, but some have taken special care to point
out the bigger flaws, particularly how the

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:42.899
film can feel exhausting or frustrating at
times.

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:45.339
The Playlist's Rodrigo Perez calls the film
...

00:00:45.339 --> 00:00:50.909
"Stress-inducing, full of dread, darker than
usual, yet, funny as hell, and super entertaining."

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:55.579
"Sure, it's a punch fest, yeah, but I thoroughly
enjoyed it, especially the dynamics of all

00:00:55.579 --> 00:00:57.219
these disparate team-ups.

00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:00.940
Let's see a Thor and Rocket Raccoon buddy
comedy spin-off."

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.390
CinemaBlend editor Eric Eisenberg felt much
the same, tweeting,

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:05.390
"Holy hell.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.750
Avengers: Infinity War is the real deal.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.040
Was left breathless sitting in the theater.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:15.200
Real consequences, stunning moments, awesome
character interaction, and some BIG and often

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.620
devastating surprises."

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.362
"I will add that it's pacing is kind of a
different beast, just because there are so

00:01:20.362 --> 00:01:21.760
many plates spinning.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.920
Excited to see it again just knowing how the
story unfolds."

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.280
Mike Ryan of UPROXXX wrote on Twitter that
the film feels like the epic opening of a

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.530
two-part film:

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:35.140
"Avengers: Infinity War has like ten legit
'chill down the spine' great moments.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:40.410
But it's for sure the first half of a two-part
movie and I left a little unsatisfied/frustrated,

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.340
maybe by design … at least that's how I
feel right now, three hours later."

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.811
The Verge's Tasha Robinson noted that Civil
War fans should be thrilled, but …

00:01:48.811 --> 00:01:54.140
"Avengers: Infinity War is basically Captain
America: Civil War times 20, except with less

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:55.530
hero-on-hero fighting.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.250
I think people who loved the airport fight
in Civil War like I did will go nuts for this

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.729
film, and people who were bored will be 20
times as bored here."

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:07.860
Bilge Ebiri of the Village Voice shared that
sentiment, writing,

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.849
"Infinity War is exhausting.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:10.989
*Exhausting*.

00:02:10.989 --> 00:02:13.530
It all just turned into noise for me after
a while."

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.870
However, Ebiri did state that the "amazing
ending almost made up for the rest of it,"

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.170
and that Thanos is a "good, sad villain",
so it appears even the not-so-great parts

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.840
of Infinity War can be balanced with the insanely
satisfying ones.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.959
But rest assured: plenty of reactions have
been completely celebratory.

00:02:29.959 --> 00:02:33.879
Take Joshua Yehl of IGN Comics, who wrote
that Avengers: Infinity War left him ...

00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:39.060
"...laughing, screaming, or crying the entire
way through […] stunned as the credits rolled,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.359
speechless and sobbing."

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:44.159
Or consider People editor Nigel M. Smith's
more subdued reaction:

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:48.510
"Avengers: Infinity War is my second fave
Marvel movie after Black Panther.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:52.069
The superhero interplay is hilarious and surprising
at every turn.

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:53.719
The action is monumental.

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:55.769
And there are actual stakes this time around.

00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:59.879
[Marvel Studios president] Kevin Feige is
not f---ing around with this one."

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:04.400
Or maybe Collider editor-in-chief Steven Weintraub's
words will resonate most:

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:05.879
"Jaw on ground.

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:08.590
You are not ready for Avengers: Infinity War.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:11.290
All you have to know is AVOID ALL SPOILERS!

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.290
Seriously.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.969
Go in knowing as little as possible.

00:03:13.969 --> 00:03:15.829
The Russo Brothers did the impossible.

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:16.829
Wow."

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:21.129
Overall, it seems people are really enjoying
Avengers: Infinity War, even if it didn't

00:03:21.129 --> 00:03:25.209
hit every mark they hoped it would, and that
the consensus is that the film is massive

00:03:25.209 --> 00:03:30.819
and moving, sometimes silly and always surprising,
and unlike anything we've ever seen in the

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:32.349
MCU before.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:36.069
Fans can formulate their own opinions when
Avengers: Infinity War arrives in theaters

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:37.370
Friday, April 27th.

